  "add-two-reversed-numbers": {
    "promptMarkdown": "Two chains of nodes each hold the digits of a whole number, **least significant digit first**. Return a new chain holding the digits of their sum, written in the same reversed order.\n\n**Constraints**\n- Each chain contains between `1` and `10^4` nodes.\n- Every node's value is a single digit between `0` and `9`.\n- The number `0` is represented by a single node with the value `0`. There are no other leading zeros (which appear as trailing zeros in the chain).\n\n**Example 1**\n```\ninput:\n2 4 3\n5 6 4\noutput: 7 0 8\n```\nThe first chain represents `342` and the second represents `465`. Their sum is `342 + 465 = 807`, which written backwards is `7 0 8`.\n\n**Example 2**\n```\ninput:\n9 9\n1\noutput: 0 0 1\n```\nThe sum of `99` and `1` is `100`, which forms `0 0 1`. The chains may have different lengths and may carry over into an extra node.\n\n**Example 3**\n```\ninput:\n0\n0\noutput: 0\n```\nAdding zero to zero results in zero.\n\n**Follow-up:** Can this be solved using O(1) extra space beyond the memory needed for the output chain itself?",
    "editorialMarkdown": "## Carry propagation over two walking pointers\n\nThe least-significant-digit-first format is actually a major advantage: it perfectly matches the right-to-left order in which manual long addition is performed. By walking both chains simultaneously from their heads and maintaining a single carry variable, the columns naturally align for the addition.\n\nThe maximum possible sum for any single column is `9 + 9 + 1 = 19`, which means the carry dividing by 10 will always be at most `1`. The overflow never cascades two columns ahead in a single step, ensuring a clean, single-pass forward traversal is sufficient without backtracking. \n\nA three-part loop condition (`while x is not None or y is not None or carry != 0:`) is the key to handling all edges gracefully. Stopping the loop when both chains run out but ignoring a remaining carry will silently truncate values like `99 + 1` into `0 0` instead of `0 0 1`. Requiring both chains to be active (using `and` instead of `or`) will truncate the sum to the length of the shorter input chain. Employing a dummy head node simplifies the construction of the result chain by avoiding a special edge case for initializing the very first node.\n\nThe time complexity is O(max(m, n)) where m and n are the lengths of the two inputs, bounded by traversing the longest chain plus at most one extra column for a final carry. The space complexity is O(1) auxiliary space beyond the O(max(m, n)) space required to allocate the nodes for the output chain.",
    "promoteSamples": [
      "0\n0"
    ],
    "model": "gemini-3.1-pro-low",
    "date": "2026-09-16"
  },
  "all-subsets-in-order": {
    "promptMarkdown": "Generate every possible subset of a given array of numbers, appearing in a strict, specific order.\n\nThe input array is **sorted from smallest to largest** and all its values are **distinct**.\n\n**The order is part of the answer.** Produce the subsets as rows formatted exactly as follows:\n1. Shorter subsets must appear before longer subsets.\n2. Among subsets of the exact same length, compare them position by position. The subset with the smaller value at the first point of difference must appear first.\n\nThe output must be a single string of rows separated by semicolons (`;`). Values within a row must be space-separated and listed in ascending order. **The empty subset is an empty row**, meaning your final output string should begin with a `;`.\n\n**Constraints**\n- The input array will contain between `1` and `8` numbers.\n- All numbers in the array are distinct.\n- The input array is given in strictly ascending order.\n\n**Example 1**\n```\ninput:\n1 2 3\noutput: ;1;2;3;1 2;1 3;2 3;1 2 3\n```\nThe output order starts with the empty subset (represented by nothing before the first `;`), followed by the three single-element subsets, the three two-element pairs in lexicographical order, and finally the complete list.\n\n**Example 2**\n```\ninput:\n5\noutput: ;5\n```\nA single-number input results in the empty subset and the number itself.\n\n**Example 3**\n```\ninput:\n1 2\noutput: ;1;2;1 2\n```\nThe empty subset, the two singles, and the pair.\n\n**Follow-up:** Can you produce this exact ordering directly through the recursion structure without applying a separate sorting step at the end?",
    "editorialMarkdown": "## Backtracking: choose, recurse, un-choose — with a shared buffer\n\nThe trick that allows the strict ordering to fall out for free is to explicitly enumerate **by size**. By iterating a target size `k` from `0` up to `n`, and only generating subsets of exactly length `k` by picking indices from left to right, the desired output order emerges naturally. Because the input arrives sorted, and the backtracking loop consumes indices in increasing order, the subsets will automatically satisfy the position-by-position requirements without needing any post-processing sort.\n\nA shared buffer array (`buf`) is reused at every level of the recursive call stack. The `pop()` operation at the end of the loop is critical: it \"un-chooses\" the element. When the recursive function returns, the buffer correctly reflects its state from before the recursive call, presenting a clean prefix for the next iteration.\n\nThe quiet mistake is appending the `buf` array directly to the output list rather than a copy of it. In languages where arrays are passed by reference, this results in the output list containing multiple pointers to the exact same underlying buffer. Because the backtracking process eventually pops the buffer back to empty on its way out, every row in the final answer will unexpectedly end up empty. You must append a clone or snapshot of the buffer.\n\nThe time complexity is O(n * 2^n), as there are 2^n subsets and copying each to the output takes up to O(n) time. Since the output itself is this large, the time is optimal. The auxiliary space complexity is O(n) for the shared recursion buffer and the call stack.",
    "promoteSamples": [
      "1 2"
    ],
    "model": "gemini-3.1-pro-low",
    "date": "2026-09-16"
  },
