  "can-split-into-equal-sized-halves": {
    "promptMarkdown": "A coach wants to split players into two teams with the **same number of players** and the **same total skill**. Given the skill ratings `a`, where every player must join exactly one team, return `true` if such a split exists and `false` otherwise.\n\nA list with an odd number of players can never be split into equal headcounts.\n\n**Constraints**\n- `1 <= a.length <= 10`\n- `0 <= a[i] <= 100`\n\n**Example 1**\n```\ninput:\n1 2 3 4\noutput: true\n```\n*Explanation: teams {1, 4} and {2, 3} both have two players and total 5.*\n\n**Example 2**\n```\ninput:\n1 1 1 3\noutput: false\n```\n*Explanation: the total is 6, but no two players sum to 3.*\n\n**Example 3**\n```\ninput:\n0\noutput: false\n```\n*Explanation: one player cannot be split into two teams of equal size.*\n\n**Follow-up:** How would you prune the search so it stays fast as the list grows?",
    "editorialMarkdown": "## Backtracking on sum and size together\n\nFirst reject the impossible cases: an odd number of players, or an odd total skill. Otherwise one team must contain exactly `n/2` players whose skills add up to half the total; the other team is everyone else.\n\nSearch with backtracking. At each index choose to put the player on the first team or not, carrying the running sum and the running count. Succeed when every player has been placed with `sum == total/2` and `count == n/2`. Because skills are non-negative, a sum above half or a count above `n/2` can never recover, so prune there.\n\nThe trap is treating this as plain subset sum and returning `true` as soon as some subset reaches half the total. That subset may have the wrong number of players, as in a split like {3} versus {1, 1, 1}.\n\nTime complexity is O(2^n). Space complexity is O(n).",
    "promoteSamples": [
      "0"
    ],
    "model": "claude-opus-5",
    "date": "2026-09-19"
  },
