    "editorialMarkdown": "The intended approach uses the fast and slow pointers pattern. While traversing the list once to count nodes and a second time to reach the middle works, it requires two passes. Instead, we can dispatch two pointers from the head: a slow pointer moving one step at a time, and a fast pointer moving two steps. By the time the fast pointer reaches the end of the chain, the slow pointer will naturally sit at the halfway mark without needing to know the list's full length. The one trap most solvers hit is writing the wrong loop condition, which dictates exactly which middle node is selected on even-length lists. Using `fast != null and fast.next != null` perfectly targets the second middle, while alternative conditions might mistakenly land on the first middle, and testing `fast.next` before `fast` can cause null pointer dereferences. The time complexity is O(n) based on the fast pointer's traversal, and space complexity is O(1) as only two pointers are allocated.",
