    "promptMarkdown": "Implement a union-find yourself; do not wrap a built-in union-find implementation.\n\nMerge several components with `unite(a,b)`. Use union by rank: attach the root with smaller rank below the larger-rank root; when ranks tie, choose either root and increase its rank. Keep path compression in `find`; `connected` and `count` must reflect every merge.\n\n**Operation log**\n\nThe first operation is the constructor. Print `null` for it and for every void method. Every other operation prints its return value.\n\nAn empty `pop` or `peek` returns `-1`.",
    "editorialMarkdown": "## Keep the forest shallow by rank\n\nRank approximates tree height. Joining the shorter root beneath the taller root avoids creating long parent chains, and only a tie can increase rank. Combined with path compression, the forest stays remarkably flat even under many mixed queries.\n\nThe quiet mistake is incrementing rank on every union rather than only equal-rank root merges; it still often connects correctly but discards the balancing guarantee being taught.\n\nUnion and find are O(alpha(n)) amortized with both optimizations; connected is two finds, count O(1), and storage is O(n).",
